An instant `histogram_quantile` over `topk by(le)` of a bare classic
bucket selector whose `le` series end at different samples. Each `le`
series answers with its own newest sample inside the lookback, so one
output series draws its rungs from two different rows; the reassembled
ladder must still be one ladder, at the evaluation instant.

The series reports bounds 1 / 2 / 4 at 23:59:00 and only 1 / 2 at
00:00:00, so the `le="4"` series ends at the older row while every other
`le` series reads the newer one:

    le:      1   2   4   +Inf
    count:  10  10   3   40

    23:59:00 row, cumulative: 1  2  3  4    (only le="4" is newest here)
    00:00:00 row, cumulative: 10 10 -  40

Prometheus repairs the non-monotonic rung at 4 up to 10. Rank
0.5 * 40 = 20 lands in the +Inf overflow bucket, which answers with the
highest finite bound: 4. Grouping the rungs by each row's own sample time
would split the ladder into two partial ones instead.

-- query.promql --
histogram_quantile(0.5, topk by(le) (5, http_server_request_duration_bucket))
-- parity --
oracle: prometheus
endpoint: /api/v1/query
scope: full
-- seed --
CREATE TABLE otel_metrics_histogram (
    MetricName String,
    Attributes Map(String, String),
    TimeUnix DateTime64(9),
    BucketCounts Array(UInt64),
    ExplicitBounds Array(Float64)
) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY (MetricName, Attributes, TimeUnix);
INSERT INTO otel_metrics_histogram VALUES
    ('http_server_request_duration', map('service', 'a'), toDateTime64('2025-12-31 23:59:00', 9), [1, 1, 1, 1], [1.0, 2.0, 4.0]),
    ('http_server_request_duration', map('service', 'a'), toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:00', 9), [10, 0, 30], [1.0, 2.0]);
-- expected_rows --
[
  ["", {"service": "a"}, "2026-01-01T00:00:01Z", 4.0]
]
